{"packet_id":"D016-01","doc_id":"D016","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"FEHA source-of-income protection for voucher holders","requirement":"A housing provider may not refuse to rent to an applicant or apply different terms, conditions or privileges because of the applicant's source of income, including use of a Section 8 Housing Choice voucher, and must comply with subsidy program paperwork and inspections.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Most housing accommodations in California, including landlords, tenant screening companies and other housing providers; single-family homes, apartments, short-term rentals, subsidized housing, shelters and mobile homes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Owner occupying the home and renting to one additional person may exclude applicants based on protected characteristics"},"exemptions":"An owner who occupies the home and rents to one additional person may exclude applicants based on protected characteristics, but may not publish discriminatory notices; shared living areas may be limited to one sex; senior housing may be limited by age.","penalty":"Remedies include out-of-pocket losses, injunction, access to the denied housing, emotional distress damages, civil penalties or punitive damages, and attorney's fees.","effective_date":null,"citation":"California Fair Employment and Housing Act (FEHA)","quoted_span":"No. California law protects tenants from discrimination based on how a tenant will be paying rent (“source of income”). This includes the State of California’s COVID-19 Rent Relief Program, Housing Choice (Section 8) vouchers, (SSI) social security disability benefits, or wages earned from a job.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D016-01","doc_id":"D016","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"FEHA limits on criminal history screening","requirement":"A housing provider may not impose blanket bans on applicants with criminal history and may not consider arrests without conviction, sealed or expunged records or juvenile records; a conviction may support denial only if directly related to the ability to be a good tenant.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Housing providers covered by FEHA in California, including landlords and third-party tenant screening companies, screening applicants for most housing accommodations.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Owner occupying the home and renting to one additional person may exclude applicants based on protected characteristics"},"exemptions":"An owner who occupies the home and rents to one additional person may exclude applicants based on protected characteristics, but may not publish discriminatory notices.","penalty":"Remedies include out-of-pocket losses, injunction, access to the denied housing, emotional distress damages, civil penalties or punitive damages, and attorney's fees.","effective_date":null,"citation":"California Fair Employment and Housing Act (FEHA)","quoted_span":"Housing  providers cannot consider some types of criminal history including arrests that did not lead to a conviction, records that are sealed or expunged, or records or matters processed in the juvenile justice system. Housing providers are also prohibited from having “blanket bans” of all people with a criminal history.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D016-01","doc_id":"D016","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No pet deposit or pet rent for service and emotional-support animals","requirement":"A housing provider must make exceptions to no-pets rules as a reasonable accommodation for a disability and may not charge pet deposits or pet rent for service animals or emotional-support animals, or apply pet breed, size or weight limits to them.","key_value":"No pet deposit or pet rent","coverage_conditions":"Housing covered by California fair housing law; tenants and applicants with disabilities who need a service animal or emotional-support animal as a reasonable accommodation.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"California Fair Employment and Housing Act (FEHA)","quoted_span":"Service animals and emotional-support animals are not subject to breed, size, or weight restrictions ordinarily applied to pets, and tenants cannot be charged pet deposits or pet rent for them.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
